VUPDATE @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-vupdate, @ARCH@) - EARLY TEST RELEASE
=========================================================================

VUPDATE keeps an OpenVMS system's VSI software up to date, like a package
manager: it compares the installed products (PRODUCT SHOW PRODUCT) with the
VSI Service Platform catalogue (sp.vmssoftware.com), lists newer kits, OpenVMS
patches and security fixes, and downloads and installs the ones you choose.
It needs a VSI portal account. It is not a VSI product.

    $ VUPDATE LOGIN                  ! once per node: your portal e-mail and password
    $ VUPDATE CHECK                  ! what is new; /SECURITY, /ALL, /OUTPUT=file
    $ VUPDATE SHOW OPENSSH           ! a kit's details: files, prerequisites, notes
    $ VUPDATE UPGRADE OPENSSH        ! download, check and install it
    $ VUPDATE UPGRADE/PATCHES/NOREBOOT   ! every patch that needs no reboot
    $ VUPDATE HELP                   ! all commands and qualifiers

Setting it up
-------------
    $ @SYS$STARTUP:VUPDATE$STARTUP.COM       ! at boot (SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM)
    $ @VUPDATE$ROOT:[COM]VUPDATE$SETUP.COM   ! in a system manager's LOGIN.COM
    $ VUPDATE LOGIN

VUPDATE is for system managers: installing needs the privileges of PRODUCT
INSTALL, and the saved credentials belong to the account that ran LOGIN.

What UPGRADE does
-----------------
For each kit chosen it adds the kits it needs first that are not installed:
those the portal lists, and those named under "KIT DEPENDENCIES" in the kit's
release notes (the portal's lists are incomplete). Kits made for another
OpenVMS version are left out. It shows the list in install order and asks once.
Then for each kit it downloads the files, checks each one's MD5 against the
portal's, unpacks the ZIP (or self-extracting .ZIPEXE) itself, and runs

    PRODUCT INSTALL kit /SOURCE=dir /SAVE_RECOVERY_DATA /OPTIONS=(SIGNED_KIT,NOCONFIRM)

on your terminal: PCSI checks VSI's signature and refuses a kit that fails;
VSI's own kit procedure asks its questions and checks its prerequisites.
Patches can be taken out again with PRODUCT UNDO PATCH. A kit that fails is
kept in VUPDATE$ROOT:[KITS] and the others go on; failed kits get a second try
at the end (VSI kits check prerequisites the portal doesn't list). Kits that
need a reboot don't stop the run: they are listed at the end, for one reboot.
VUPDATE never installs an OpenVMS upgrade; it reports one.

Patch recovery data: installing any full kit (a product update such as
OPENSSH, not a patch) makes PCSI delete the saved recovery data of every
patch, so PRODUCT UNDO PATCH can no longer take them out. VUPDATE installs
full kits before patches where it can, lists the patches that would lose
their recovery data and asks, even with /NOCONFIRM; in a batch job it
installs no full kit while recovery data exists unless /DISCARD_RECOVERY_DATA
is given. The same applies to installing any other full kit by hand, this
one included.

Nightly check
-------------
    $ @VUPDATE$ROOT:[COM]VUPDATE_NIGHTLY START [hh:mm]   ! default 02:00
    $ @VUPDATE$ROOT:[COM]VUPDATE_NIGHTLY STOP
    $ @VUPDATE$ROOT:[COM]VUPDATE_NIGHTLY NOW             ! one check now

A batch job (SYS$BATCH, or the queue in VUPDATE$NIGHTLY_QUEUE) under your
account runs VUPDATE CHECK every night and queues itself for the next. When the
findings change it sends an operator message (REQUEST) and, if the logical
VUPDATE$NOTIFY_MAIL is an address, mails the report. It never installs.

The saved credentials
---------------------
VUPDATE LOGIN tests the e-mail and password with VSI's sign-in service and
saves them in VUPDATE$ROOT:[DATA]CREDENTIALS.DAT, readable by its owner only.
The contents are sealed (AES-256-GCM) with a key derived from this node's
identity, so the file is useless on another node and a modified file is
refused. This is NOT secrecy from privileged users: anyone who can read the
file on this node can recover the password. VUPDATE LOGOUT deletes it, as
does PRODUCT REMOVE VUPDATE. A changed SCSNODE means VUPDATE LOGIN again.

Logical names
-------------
    VUPDATE$ROOT          the installation ([SYSEXE] [COM] [HELP] [DATA] [CACHE] [KITS])
    VUPDATE$PROXY         a proxy URL for all VUPDATE connections
    VUPDATE$CA_FILE       another CA bundle than [DATA]CACERT.PEM
    VUPDATE$CREDENTIALS   another credential file
    VUPDATE$NIGHTLY_TIME, VUPDATE$NIGHTLY_QUEUE, VUPDATE$NOTIFY_MAIL  (nightly check)

Requires
--------
OpenVMS x86-64 (V9.2 or later) or Alpha (V8.4-2L2), VSI SSL3 (OpenSSL 3), a VSI
Service Platform account, and HTTPS access to sp.vmssoftware.com,
kc.teracloud.com and the portal's download store (nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com).

Limits
------
The portal's interface is VSI's and not documented: if it changes, VUPDATE
says "unexpected reply" rather than guessing. The portal's "restart required"
flag is not always right (kits say so themselves when they install). Release
notes that come only as PDF can't be read for dependencies. Portal accounts
with multi-factor sign-in are not supported.

